cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "DEGALI: Dense Gas Dispersion for Liquid Hydrogen"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software

# Required by CFF 1.2.0 and by Zenodo. This file previously had no `authors`
# block at all, which Zenodo rejects; it is the kind of thing that is found at
# the moment of publication rather than before it.
#
# Zenodo's GitHub integration assigns the DOI after the first GitHub release.
# Add that DOI in the first post-release metadata update; never copy a DOI from
# another project or version into this file.
authors:
  - family-names: "Lee"
    given-names: "Ugwiyeon"
    affiliation: "Korea Gas Safety Corporation"
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"

version: "0.1.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22646259"
date-released: 2026-09-07

abstract: >-
  DEGALI (Dense Gas Dispersion for Liquid Hydrogen) reimplements DEGADIS 2.1,
  the dense gas dispersion model of Spicer and Havens, in Python. The port was
  validated against a controlled local build of the original FORTRAN, at full
  double precision, through purpose-written probes rather than against printed
  output. The original FORTRAN is not redistributed. DEGALI is separately
  evaluated against the Risoe REDIPHEM database of heavy-gas release field
  trials, against the SMEDIS model-evaluation spreadsheets, and against the
  PRESLHY E3.5 liquid hydrogen campaign; the liquid hydrogen configuration adds
  equations of state, a buoyant closure and a flashing source term to the
  ported model, each switchable so that the reproduction claim is unaffected.
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/lyullee/degali"
keywords:
  - atmospheric dispersion
  - dense gas
  - heavy gas
  - LNG
  - liquid hydrogen
  - process safety
  - consequence modelling
  - DEGADIS
references:
  - type: report
    title: "User's Guide for the DEGADIS 2.1 Dense Gas Dispersion Model"
    authors:
      - family-names: Spicer
        given-names: Thomas O.
      - family-names: Havens
        given-names: Jerry A.
    institution:
      name: US Environmental Protection Agency
    number: EPA-450/4-89-019
    year: 1989

  # The title and the expansion of the acronym were both wrong here. It read
  # 'REDIPHEM: Rise Data on Heavy Gas Dispersion', which is not the title of
  # anything: REDIPHEM is the CEC DG XII ENVIRONMENT project the database was
  # produced under, and the report is Nielsen and Ott's. This project's own
  # reference audit sets the standard that a citation is checked against the
  # source rather than reconstructed from memory; this one had not been.
  - type: report
    title: "A collection of data from dense gas experiments"
    authors:
      - family-names: Nielsen
        given-names: Morten
      - family-names: Ott
        given-names: Soren
    institution:
      name: Risoe National Laboratory, Roskilde
    number: "Risoe-R-845(EN)"
    year: 1995
    notes: >-
      The REDIPHEM database, produced under the CEC DG XII ENVIRONMENT project
      of that name. Widely cited with a 1996 date, which is the announcement
      year; the report itself is 1995.

  - type: data
    title: "PRESLHY Deliverable D3.5: liquid hydrogen release and dispersion experiments, raw data"
    institution:
      name: Karlsruhe Institute of Technology
    doi: "10.35097/1481"
    year: 2021
